section.para:
			Annotate a document, read the layer, then produce a redacted copy in which
			only detected spans are replaced. Length is preserved: every character
			inside a mention becomes the mask character, and everything outside stays
			unchanged. PiiUsageExampleTest asserts the behavior shown
			here.

String text =
    "Contact jane@example.com, call (555) 123-4567, or charge card 4111 1111 1111 1111.";

Document document = new PiiAnnotator(new CursorPiiExtractor())
    .annotate(Document.of(text));

List<Annotation<PiiMention>> mentions = document.get(PiiAnnotator.PII);
// email, phone, card: exact spans, types, and normalized forms

String masked = Masker.mask(document, PiiAnnotator.PII, '*');
// "Contact ****************, call **************, or charge card *******************."

			An ASCII email candidate is rejected if the domain continues into a
			Unicode letter or digit, including after dots or hyphens. A terminal dot
			or hyphen is excluded from the span. The scanner does not convert Unicode
			domain names to punycode. EmailSpanTest checks this example:

String emailText = "Skip user@example.com.é; contact User+tag@Example.COM.";
Document emailDocument = new PiiAnnotator(PiiPacks.contact())
    .annotate(Document.of(emailText));
String emailMasked = Masker.mask(emailDocument, PiiAnnotator.PII, '*');
// "Skip user@example.com.é; contact " + "*".repeat("User+tag@Example.COM".length()) + "."
String emailReplaced = new Pseudonymizer().rewrite(emailDocument).text();
// "Skip user@example.com.é; contact EMAIL-1."

			A phone span includes its formatting, including a closing parenthesis
			paired with an opening parenthesis inside the number. Normalization
			removes that formatting. The following period is outside the span, so
			masking and label replacement leave it in place.
			PhoneSpanTest checks this example:

String phone = "+1 (4155550123)";
Document phoneDocument = new PiiAnnotator(PiiPacks.contact())
    .annotate(Document.of("Call " + phone + "."));
String phoneMasked = Masker.mask(phoneDocument, PiiAnnotator.PII, '*');
// "Call " + "*".repeat(phone.length()) + "."
String phoneReplaced = new Pseudonymizer().rewrite(phoneDocument).text();
// "Call PHONE-1."

			An international candidate starts with + and an assigned
			calling code without leading zeros. For example, +01 is
			not treated as +1. Zeros within the national number are
			preserved. InternationalPhonePrefixTest checks this example:

Document internationalPhones = new PiiAnnotator(PiiPacks.contact())
    .annotate(Document.of("Invalid +014155550123; call +14155550123."));
String internationalMasked = Masker.mask(internationalPhones, PiiAnnotator.PII, '*');
// "Invalid +014155550123; call ************."

			Card candidates have 13 to 19 digits and pass the Luhn checksum. Their
			accepted prefixes are 2 through 6 and
			UnionPay's
			81 prefix. Single spaces or hyphens may separate digits,
			including the prefix digits. CardPrefixTest checks this
			synthetic number and the receipt-style output:

Document cards = new PiiAnnotator(PiiPacks.payment())
    .annotate(Document.of("Card 8100 0000 0000 0028."));
String cardReceipt = Masker.mask(cards, PiiAnnotator.PII, MaskPolicies.byType());
// "Card **** **** **** 0028."

			IBANs are recognized in compact form or with spaces between groups
			after the 4-character country/check-digit header. Normalization removes
			those spaces; masking covers the complete original span. Country, length
			and checksum checks do not establish whether an account exists.
			IbanValidationTest checks this example:

String account = "DE89 3704 0044 0532 0130 00";
Document transfer = new PiiAnnotator(PiiPacks.payment())
    .annotate(Document.of("Wire " + account + "; reference 1234."));
String maskedTransfer = Masker.mask(transfer, PiiAnnotator.PII, '*');
// "Wire " + "*".repeat(account.length()) + "; reference 1234."
String replacedTransfer = new Pseudonymizer().rewrite(transfer).text();
// "Wire IBAN-1; reference 1234."

			Masking accepts positional layers only. A document-scoped layer, such as
			a language identifier, has no spans and is rejected with
			IllegalArgumentException. Each policy reads the original
			text. When spans overlap, a character remains masked if either span
			redacts it. Layer order does not affect the result when using one policy:

LayerKey<String> left = LayerKey.of("left", String.class);
LayerKey<String> right = LayerKey.of("right", String.class);
Document overlaps = Document.of("123456")
    .with(left, List.of(new Annotation<>(new Span(0, 4), "1234")))
    .with(right, List.of(new Annotation<>(new Span(2, 6), "3456")));

String masked = Masker.mask(overlaps, List.of(right, left),
    MaskPolicy.of('*').keepingTrailing(2));
// "****56" with either layer order

listitem.para:
					CryptoPiiExtractor: Bitcoin addresses, legacy
					Base58Check and segwit bech32 or bech32m, and Ethereum addresses.
					Bitcoin candidates require a valid checksum and mainnet encoding;
					testnet and regtest forms are excluded. Segwit versions 0 through 16
					are supported, including program-length and padding checks.
					Ethereum candidates require 0x or 0X and
					40 ASCII hexadecimal digits. Mixed-case values require the
					EIP-55 checksum;
					single-case values are accepted without that check. The zero address
					is excluded. An Ethereum address does not identify its network.
					Detection does not verify ownership, use or balances, and single-case
					hexadecimal identifiers may be false positives.
					Word boundaries use Unicode letters and digits; punctuation, including
					underscores, separates candidates. Normalization preserves Base58 case,
					lowercases segwit text and applies EIP-55 capitalization to Ethereum.
					Masking uses the original text and offsets:

String address = "0X5AAEB6053F3E94C9B9A09F33669435E7EF1BEAED";
Document result = new PiiAnnotator(PiiPacks.crypto()).annotate(
    Document.of("Address: " + address + "."));
String normalized = result.get(PiiAnnotator.PII).getFirst().value().normalized();
// "0x5aAeb6053F3E94C9b9A09f33669435E7Ef1BeAed"
String masked = Masker.mask(result, PiiAnnotator.PII, '*');
// "Address: " + "*".repeat(address.length()) + "."

section.para:
			National identity detectors are opt-in. A match checks format and number
			rules, not assignment to a person. This example selects the European
			identity pack and masks the original grouped spans:

Document identifiers = new PiiAnnotator(PiiPacks.euIdentity())
    .annotate(Document.of("NHS: 943 476 5919; IdNr: 65-929-970-489."));
String masked = Masker.mask(identifiers, PiiAnnotator.PII, '*');
// "NHS: ************; IdNr: **************."

			SIN and IMEI labels are ASCII case-insensitive and must not continue a
			Unicode letter or digit. Whitespace and :, # or
			= may separate labels from values; SIN also permits
			.. Both detectors check format and checksum, not assignment
			to a person or device. This example masks only the numeric spans:

PiiExtractor labeled = new CompositePiiExtractor(PiiPacks.caIdentity(), PiiPacks.device());
Document identifiers = new PiiAnnotator(labeled)
    .annotate(Document.of("SIN: 046 454 286; IMEI: 490154203237518."));
String masked = Masker.mask(identifiers, PiiAnnotator.PII, '*');
// "SIN: ***********; IMEI: ***************."

			This synthetic routing number illustrates extraction and masking without
			using a bank-directory entry:

Document payment = new PiiAnnotator(PiiPacks.payment())
    .annotate(Document.of("Routing 001234561."));
String masked = Masker.mask(payment, PiiAnnotator.PII, '*');
// "Routing *********."

CompositePiiExtractor runs several extractors over one text and merges the result. PiiPacks provides payment(), contact(), network(), secrets(), crypto(), usIdentity(), euIdentity(), caIdentity(), device(), and allStructured() for all supported structured types. Overlapping mentions retain their original types, normalized values and offsets. Results are ordered by start offset, descending span length, PiiTypePriority rank, then delegate order. Equal mentions, as defined by PiiMention.equals, are combined using the first original object. Nested composites contribute their individual extractors in depth-first, left-to-right order.
String text = "Deploy from 10.0.0.7 using AKIAIOSFODNN7EXAMPLE, ask jane@example.com.";
List<PiiMention> mentions = PiiPacks.allStructured().extract(text); // ipv4, aws-access-key, email
PiiExtractor keysOnly = PiiPacks.secrets(); PiiExtractor custom = new CompositePiiExtractor( new CursorPiiExtractor(), new NetworkPiiExtractor());

			A context-labeled IMEI can also match the payment-card detector.
			Both detections are retained. IMEI sorts before card regardless of pack order.
			PiiPacksTest checks this example:

PiiExtractor deviceAndPayment =
    new CompositePiiExtractor(PiiPacks.payment(), PiiPacks.device());
Document deviceDocument = new PiiAnnotator(deviceAndPayment)
    .annotate(Document.of("IMEI: 490154203237518."));
List<String> types = deviceDocument.get(PiiAnnotator.PII).stream()
    .map(annotation -> annotation.value().type()).toList();
// List.of("imei", "card")
String deviceMasked = Masker.mask(deviceDocument, PiiAnnotator.PII, '*');
// "IMEI: ***************."

			Grouping the same ordered extractors into nested composites does not
			change their result. This example retains all 3 mentions and masks their
			combined spans, including the space:

PiiExtractor first = text -> List.of(new PiiMention(new Span(0, 3), "person", "Amy"));
PiiExtractor middle = text -> List.of(new PiiMention(new Span(1, 5), "custom", "my B"));
PiiExtractor last = text -> List.of(new PiiMention(new Span(4, 7), "person", "Bob"));
PiiExtractor combined = new CompositePiiExtractor(first,
    new CompositePiiExtractor(middle, last));
Document people = new PiiAnnotator(combined).annotate(Document.of("Amy Bob"));
String masked = Masker.mask(people, PiiAnnotator.PII, '*');
// "*******"

			Adding detectors preserves the coverage of existing detections. In this
			URL, the credentials and email overlap. Both remain available, so full
			masking covers the credentials and the email domain:

String address = "https://john:pass@example.com/path";
Document link = new PiiAnnotator(PiiPacks.allStructured()).annotate(Document.of(address));
List<PiiMention> matches = link.get(PiiAnnotator.PII).stream()
    .map(annotation -> annotation.value()).toList();
// url-credential [8, 17), email [13, 29)
String maskedLink = Masker.mask(link, PiiAnnotator.PII, '*');
// "https://*********************/path"

			Pseudonymizer replaces mentions with numbered labels. Mentions
			with the same type and normalized value use the same label within a text.
			PiiRewrite provides the rewritten text, label locations and
			offset mapping.

String text = "Contact jane@example.com; jane@example.com replied to bob@example.com.";

PiiRewrite rewrite = new Pseudonymizer()
    .rewrite(text, new CursorPiiExtractor().extract(text));
// "Contact EMAIL-1; EMAIL-1 replied to EMAIL-2."

int moved = rewrite.mapOffset(text.indexOf("bob@example.com"));

Document labelled = Document.of(rewrite.text()).with(PiiAnnotator.PII,
    rewrite.mentions().stream()
        .map(mention -> new Annotation<>(mention.span(), mention)).toList());

			Types are case-sensitive. Labels uppercase ASCII letters in the type and
			share a numbering sequence when the displayed prefixes match. Custom
			types such as id and ID receive distinct labels.
			Numbering starts at one for each rewrite, so matching labels in separate
			documents do not identify the same value.

List<PiiMention> custom = List.of(
    new PiiMention(new Span(0, 1), "id", "a"),
    new PiiMention(new Span(2, 3), "ID", "b"));
String numbered = new Pseudonymizer().rewrite("a b", custom).text();
// "ID-1 ID-2"

			HmacTokenizer derives a token from the type and normalized value
			under a secret key. The same input and key produce the same token across
			documents. Use at least 32 random key bytes and keep the key separate from
			the data. Retain the key to compare tokens across runs. Tokens are linkable
			pseudonymous data and require access control.

byte[] key = new byte[32];
new SecureRandom().nextBytes(key);
HmacTokenizer tokenizer = new HmacTokenizer(key);

PiiAnnotator annotator = new PiiAnnotator(new CursorPiiExtractor());
Document first = annotator.annotate(Document.of("From jane@example.com"));
Document second = annotator.annotate(Document.of("To jane@example.com"));
String stable = tokenizer.token(PiiMention.TYPE_EMAIL, "jane@example.com");

String from = tokenizer.rewrite(first).text();   // "From " + stable
String to = tokenizer.rewrite(second).text();   // "To " + stable

PiiAuditReport reports mention counts, distinct-value counts for retained detections, including overlaps, and up to three sampled tokens per type by default. It stores types, counts and tokens, without normalized values or offsets. Custom types should be labels, not personal data, because they appear in the report. Tokens remain linkable pseudonymous data.
PiiAuditReport report = PiiAuditReport.of(first, tokenizer);
report.total(); // 1 report.counts().get(PiiMention.TYPE_EMAIL); // 1 report.distinctCounts().get(PiiMention.TYPE_EMAIL); // 1 report.samples(PiiMention.TYPE_EMAIL); // List.of(stable)

toString() produces one line per type. It doubles backslashes and escapes colons, commas, square brackets, control characters, Unicode formatting characters, line and paragraph separators, and unpaired surrogates as UTF-16 hexadecimal sequences. The types and tokens returned by accessors are unchanged.
List<PiiMention> customMentions = List.of( new PiiMention(new Span(0, 1), "custom\nid", "a")); PiiAuditReport customReport = PiiAuditReport.of(customMentions, tokenizer, 0); String line = customReport.toString();
The output is custom\u000aid: 1 mentions, 1 distinct on one line. This escaping is for plain-text reports, not HTML or JSON output encoding.
